sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Systems made of discrete components appear smooth and continuous on large scales mainly because microscopic randomness averages out the discreteness, and it does not matter whether that randomness comes from outside or from intrinsic generation, as shown by random walks, aggregation models and deterministic cellular automata that grow circular shapes.";
    dc:type "computational-observation";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Randomness lets discrete components produce continuous behavior";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Phenomenon of Continuity” (from p. 327), p. 333. Scope: discrete systems such as random walks, aggregation models and cellular automata, and fluids made of molecules"
}
